# Unattended root for the study's privileged conditions.
#
# INSTALL (JC does this; the agent does not touch sudoers):
#     sudo install -m 0440 -o root -g wheel \
#         Study/launchd/netsanity-study.sudoers /etc/sudoers.d/netsanity-study
#     sudo visudo -c -f /etc/sudoers.d/netsanity-study     # syntax check
#
# REMOVE WHEN COLLECTION ENDS:
#     sudo rm /etc/sudoers.d/netsanity-study
#
# This file is part of the method and goes in the reproducibility appendix, per
# PHASE0.md's outstanding decision on unattended root and HARNESS.md's repeat of it.
#
# ── Why NOPASSWD and not a Touch ID prompt ───────────────────────────────────────
#
# Touch ID was tried on 2026-09-12 and works: `sudo true` succeeds with a fingerprint.
# It does not help. `run_block.py` invokes `sudo -n`, and `-n` means "fail rather than
# prompt" — which is the correct thing for a harness that must not sit on a prompt for
# seven days, and which no PAM configuration can satisfy because there is nobody at the
# machine at 04:00 to touch anything. The choice is not between a password and a
# fingerprint; it is between an unattended grant and no overnight strata.
#
# ── Why three binaries and not a blanket rule ────────────────────────────────────
#
#   nmap     the four privileged timing-sweep conditions (H1, H2)
#   tcpdump  the per-run packet capture, which EVERY condition needs — the capture is
#            the oracle that separates a scanner miss from a sleeping device, and a run
#            without it is not evidence
#   arp      the per-block neighbour-table flush; cache state is a condition and not an
#            accident, so a block that cannot flush is aborted rather than recorded warm
#
# netsanity is deliberately NOT here. It was, in the draft written before gate 0.3 ran.
# H3 was withdrawn on 2026-09-12 because netsanity has no privileged engine, so there is
# no privileged netsanity condition left to grant anything to. Granting it anyway would
# be a standing root capability for a binary nothing needs to run as root.
#
# ── What this does not narrow, stated rather than glossed ────────────────────────
#
# These are whole-binary grants, so they permit any arguments, and `nmap` as root with
# arbitrary arguments is a broad capability on this machine for as long as the file is
# installed. Argument-matching was considered and rejected: the harness's argv carries
# per-run paths that change every run, so a pattern tight enough to be worth having
# would have to be loosened until it was not, and a rule that looks narrow while
# matching everything is worse than one that is honestly wide. The mitigations are that
# it names one user, three absolute paths, and a removal step — and that the study has
# an end date.

jc ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /opt/homebrew/bin/nmap, /usr/sbin/tcpdump, /usr/sbin/arp
